The `prfc-connect` repository implements a clear and organized structure for routing and testing team member pages.

### Team Member Page Routing

Team member pages are routed using the Next.js App Router's file-system based routing. Each team member has a dedicated page file located under the `src/app/team/` directory. The structure is as follows:

*   **`src/app/team/[member_name]/page.tsx`**: Each file in this pattern defines a specific team member's page. For example, the page for Karson Chen is defined in `src/app/team/chen/page.tsx` (lines 1-14).


```typescript
    // src/app/team/chen/page.tsx
    export default function ChenPage() {
      return (
        <div className="min-h-screen bg-background p-8">
          <div className="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
            <h1 className="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground">Karson Chen</h1>
            <p className="mt-2 text-lg text-muted-foreground">Developer</p>
            <div className="mt-6">
              <h2 className="text-xl font-semibold">Fun Fact</h2>
              <p className="mt-2 text-muted-foreground"> I was born in New York</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      );
    }
    ```


    This setup means that accessing a URL like `/team/chen` will render the content defined in `src/app/team/chen/page.tsx`.

### Team Member API Routing

In addition to the static pages, there are API routes for managing member data:

*   **`src/app/api/members/route.ts`**: This file handles requests to the `/api/members` endpoint. The `GET` function (lines 7-41) in this file is responsible for retrieving a list of all members. It incorporates rate limiting and session verification before calling `getAllMembers()` to fetch the data.


```typescript
    // src/app/api/members/route.ts
    export async function GET(req: NextRequest) {
      try {
        // ... rate limiting logic ...
        await verifySession();
        const members = await getAllMembers();
        return NextResponse.json(members, {
          status: 200,
          headers: {
            "Cache-Control": "private, no-store",
          },
        });
      } catch (error) {
        return apiErrorHandler(error);
      }
    }
    ```


*   **`src/app/api/members/[id]/route.ts`**: This file handles requests to dynamic member API endpoints, such as `/api/members/123`. The `GET` function (lines 7-52) in this file retrieves a specific member by their ID. It also includes rate limiting, session verification, and validation of the `id` parameter before calling `getMemberById()`.


```typescript
    // src/app/api/members/[id]/route.ts
    export async function GET(req: NextRequest, { params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }) {
      try {
        // ... rate limiting logic ...
        await verifySession();
        const { id } = await params;
        if (!/^\\d+$/.test(id)) {
          throw new AppError("VALIDATION_ERROR", "Invalid member ID format");
        }
        const memberId = parseInt(id, 10);
        const member = await getMemberById(memberId);
        // ... error handling for member not found ...
        return NextResponse.json(member, {
          status: 200,
          headers: {
            "Cache-Control": "private, no-store",
          },
        });
      } catch (error) {
        return apiErrorHandler(error);
      }
    }
    ```


### Team Member Page Test Organization

The tests for the individual team member pages are organized in the `test/team/` directory, mirroring the structure of the `src/app/team/` directory:

*   **`test/team/[member_name].test.tsx`**: Each file in this pattern contains unit tests for the corresponding team member's page component. For example, `test/team/chen.test.tsx` (lines 1-15) tests the `ChenPage` component.


```typescript
    // test/team/chen.test.tsx
    import { render, screen } from "@testing-library/react";
    import Page from "@/app/team/chen/page";

    describe("Chen Team Page", () => {
      it("renders name and role", () => {
        render(<Page />);
        expect(screen.getByText("Karson Chen")).toBeInTheDocument();
        expect(screen.getByText("Developer")).toBeInTheDocument();
      });

      it("renders fun fact section", () => {
        render(<Page />);
        expect(screen.getByText("Fun Fact")).toBeInTheDocument();
      });
    });
    ```


    These tests utilize `@testing-library/react` to render the page component and assert that specific text content (like the member's name, role, and "Fun Fact" heading) is present in the rendered output. This approach ensures that the components correctly display the expected information.

